 <h1>Exercise Name: Half Crunches (also known as Abdominal Crunches or Basic Crunches).</h1>
  <u><b><h4>Video Overview:</h4></b></u>
  <pre>The 3D anatomical animation shows a model lying on their back on an exercise mat with knees bent and
       feet flat on the floor. The hands are held near the head with elbows pointing forward. The upper torso
       lifts slightly off the ground, flexing the spine to contract the upper and middle sections of the
       rectus abdominis, which are highlighted in red and yellow to demonstrate active target muscle engagement.
     
</div>
</div>
<pre>
<h1> Step-by-Step How to Perform</h1>

<u><b><h4>1. Starting Position:</h4></b></u>Lie flat on your back on an exercise mat. Bend your knees at a 90-degree angle with your feet flat on the floor, about hip-width apart. Place your hands lightly behind or near your head with your elbows pointing upward/forward.
<u><b><h4>2. Engage the Core:</h4></b></u> Press your lower back flat into the mat by pulling your belly button toward your spine.
<u><b><h4>3. Curl Upward:</h4></b></u> Exhale as you contract your abs, lifting only your head, neck, and shoulder blades off the mat. Focus on bringing your ribcage down toward your hips rather than pulling yourself all the way up.
<u><b><h4>4. Pause at the Top:</h4></b></u> Hold the peak contraction briefly at the top of the movement while keeping tension on your abdominal muscles.
<u><b><h4>5. Lower with Control:</h4></b></u> Inhale as you slowly lower your upper body back down until your shoulder blades lightly touch the mat before starting the next repetition.


<h1>Key Benefits</h1>

 <b>◉Targeted Upper Abs:</b> Focuses intensely on the upper portion of the rectus abdominis without involving heavy leg or hip involvement.
 <b>◉Lower Back Friendly:</b> Unlike full sit-ups, half crunches keep the lumbar spine supported against the mat, reducing strain on the lower back.
 <b>◉Core Isolation: Isolates</b> the abdominal wall effectively by eliminating momentum and minimizing hip flexor assistance.
 <b>◉Beginner-Friendly Base:</b> Excellent foundation movement for building core stability and endurance.



<h1> Reps, Sets & Recommendations</h1>

| Level | Reps Per Set | Sets | Rest Period |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Beginner | 12 – 15 reps | 3 Sets | 45 – 60 seconds |
| Intermediate | 15 – 20 reps | 3 – 4 Sets | 45 seconds |
| Advanced | 20 – 25+ reps | 4 Sets | 30 – 45 seconds |

> Tip: Keep your eyes looking diagonally upward at the ceiling to avoid tucking your chin into your chest or pulling on your neck with your hands.
